# Caddy — the same-origin OIDC redirect_uri callback for the authentik branch (identity-provider · GLA-074).
#
# This is a TEMPLATE snippet (the lower-trust payload tier): the executing agent fills the ⟨…⟩ placeholders for
# THIS host, then MERGES it into the operator's existing GLA site block (the one edge-proxy wrote — see
# ../../../edge-proxy/payload/templates/Caddyfile.tmpl). Reload, don't replace; the receipt records the checksum
# of what was written and the inverse op (remove this route / restore the prior Caddyfile, reload Caddy).
#
# WHY this exists (authentik-service-standup.md §5, authentik-dual-method-flow.md §5.2). The OIDC step-up is a
# top-level redirect: GLA's page → authentik's hosted login → BACK to GLA. The return MUST land on **GLA's own
# origin** (the gateway, the sole public entry on :3000), so:
#   • the callback page's SAME-ORIGIN state works (it restores the GLA grant from same-origin sessionStorage and
#     re-POSTs {code,state} to the gateway's UNCHANGED /handoff/auth/verify or /enroll/verify); and
#   • the **GLA grant never travels to authentik** — only the OIDC code/state flow to/from authentik; the grant
#     is confined to GLA's origin (page → callback → verify route). authentik sees the code/state, never the grant.
#
# So the redirect_uri host = GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL's host (NOT authentik's host), and the path resolves to a
# GLA-served page (the delegated-auth callback landing route). Because the GLA site block already
# reverse-proxies EVERY path to GLA's :3000, the callback path is reachable through that same upstream — this
# snippet just pins the path↔origin agreement explicitly and documents the security property. The callback is on
# GLA's origin, NOT authentik's, and is NOT the local Agent Bridge (the bridge stays local — the S-6 guard).
#
# Set GLA_AUTHENTIK_REDIRECT_URI to exactly this URL, and register it as the authentik app's allowed redirect URI
# (authentik exact-matches it). Examples:
#   root:    GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://gla.example/
#            GLA_AUTHENTIK_REDIRECT_URI=https://gla.example/auth/callback
#   subpath: GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://gla.example/team-a/
#            GLA_AUTHENTIK_REDIRECT_URI=https://gla.example/team-a/auth/callback
#   issuer:  GLA_AUTHENTIK_ISSUER_URL=https://idp.example/application/o/gla/  (may be a separate origin)
# hermes-1 root example: https://57.131.31.126/auth/callback
#
# ── MERGE this inside the GLA public site block (the `⟨gla.example.com⟩ { … }` from the edge-proxy Caddyfile) ──
#
#   ⟨gla.example.com⟩ {
#       # The OIDC redirect_uri callback — SAME-ORIGIN with the gateway. It resolves to GLA's :3000 upstream like
#       # every other path, so the GLA-served callback page runs here and re-POSTs
#       # {code,state} to the unchanged verify route. The GLA grant is never in this request to authentik.
#       handle ⟨/auth/callback⟩ {
#           reverse_proxy ⟨127.0.0.1:13000⟩   # the SAME upstream that reaches GLA's gateway :3000 on this topology
#       }
#
#       # … the existing `reverse_proxy ⟨127.0.0.1:13000⟩` for everything else stays as edge-proxy wrote it …
#   }
#
# For a subpath GLA base (`/team-a`), merge the callback under the same prefix as the rest of GLA. If Caddy strips
# the prefix with `handle_path /team-a/*`, set `header_up X-Forwarded-Prefix /team-a` before the `reverse_proxy`
# and set `GLA_TRUST_FORWARDED_PREFIX=true` only behind this sanitizing edge; if an existing proxy preserves
# `/team-a`, forward it unchanged and leave the trust flag false. In both modes the callback still lands on GLA.
#
# authentik's OWN endpoints (issuer/authorize/token/jwks) are fronted SEPARATELY — at the operator's authentik
# origin (its own host/subdomain), proxied to the host-level authentik stack (§5.1). They are NOT part of this
# snippet; GLA only needs to be able to DIAL them over the network (record a container-reachable issuer, §8).
#
# If the operator also uses authentik proxy/forward-auth, keep that as a separate outer-guard route (typically
# `/outpost.goauthentik.io/*` plus the reverse proxy's forward-auth directive). The callback here still lands on
# GLA's gateway and GLA still verifies the grant/recipient/assurance decision; an outpost session is not a GLA
# grant and cannot bypass `/handoff/auth/*` or `/enroll/verify`.
#
# NOTE: the callback path is served by GLA's gateway, not by authentik. A verify step should open the chosen
# public callback URL and see the GLA callback page, then prove the original GLA grant stays in same-origin
# storage and never appears in the authentik authorize/redirect URI.
